== (1) Pisano facts and the GL_2 decode of 60 ==
  pi(2) = 3
  pi(5) = 20
  pi(10) = 60
  det M = -1  (unimodular: the linear plane 'Keller' map)
  order of M in GL_2(F_2) = 3;  |GL_2(F_2)| = 6, GL_2(F_2) iso S_3  => M mod 2 = a 3-CYCLE in S_3
  (x-3)^2 - (x^2-x-1) = 10 - 5*x: all coeffs divisible by 5: True  => 5 RAMIFIED in Q(sqrt5), double eigenvalue 3
  order of M in GL_2(F_5) = 20 = 4*5 (ord(3)=4 x ramification 5)
  M^5 = 3*I mod 5  => PROJECTIVE order of M in PGL_2(F_5) = 5 (an order-5 'pentagon' element)
  |SL_2(F_5)| = 5*(25-1) = 120, PSL_2(F_5) iso A_5 (order 60) -- klein's three sixties (T1534)
  60 = lcm(3, 20): a 3-cycle in S_3 (mod 2) and a dressed 5-cycle (mod 5); A_5 = <3-rot, 5-rot> (icosahedron)

== (2) the mechanism bridge: Lucas tripling == T_3 ==
  L_(3n) - (L_n^3 - 3(-1)^n L_n) == 0: True   (alpha*beta = -1)
  even n: L_(3n)/2 = T_3(L_n/2): T_3(w) = 4w^3-3w; L^3-3L = 2*T_3(L/2): True
  F_(3n) = 5F_n^3 + 3(-1)^n F_n: True
  T_3(T_3(w)) == T_9(w) (index-tripling semigroup = the F -> F.F -> ... iteration ladder, deg 3^m): True
  => THM-1335's modulus law 4T^3-3T = m and the Lucas/Fibonacci tripling laws are THE SAME identity;
     F's fibers trisect the modulus angle exactly as index-tripling trisects Fibonacci/Lucas indices.

